	Changelog

Newest first. Tagging v* is what fires .github/workflows/release.yml.


unreleased
----------

android/app: the view now answers the terminal queries the guest blocks on -- DSR
cursor position (`CSI 6 n`), status (`CSI 5 n`) and device attributes (`CSI c`).
It used to swallow them on the theory that the emulator replies; nothing does,
because src/ui/ui.c parses no escapes and a serial console has no terminal at the
other end.  A guest that asks and gets nothing waits for the answer, and the first
keystrokes after the question are consumed as that answer instead of reaching the
line editor -- the stray characters and dropped letters on the prompt were this,
on top of the input-loss fix.  Verified by compile and by the CI APK checks; the
reply path is only exercised on a device.

.github/workflows/rootfs.yml: clear the last-change date from every /etc/shadow
entry after chpasswd.  pam_unix rejects an account whose password is dated in the
future, and on a CI-built image the guest clock (no RTC) is always behind the
runner that stamped the file, which is why the console reported unix_chkpwd:
password check failed for user (root) for the correct password.  An empty field
means no aging information at all, so the check cannot depend on the clock, and
the assertion in the step keeps it that way.

src/devices/net_user.c: the NAT queue is 256 frames deep and the send window is
the largest this handshake can express (65280 bytes, since the relay does not
negotiate window scaling).  Correctness came with the tail buffer above; speed
comes with this, because a phone spends seconds per round trip and a 16 KiB
window turned a full Packages.gz into hours of small steps.  The hold-off limit
moved with the queue so the window, not the queue, is what paces a transfer.

src/devices/net_user.c, virtio_net.c, net_user.h: the userspace NAT now does flow
control and retransmission, so a bulk download finishes.  The relay used to take
whatever recv() returned, hand it to the guest and forget it: if the virtio-net RX
ring had no descriptor posted, `net_output_cb` counted a drop and the frame was
gone, and if the 48-frame queue in front of it was full, `qrx` returned without
queueing.  Nothing ever re-sent those bytes, so any transfer longer than a few
dozen frames stalled forever -- a DNS answer or a 22 KB Release file worked, while
`apt-get update` (which fetches megabytes) hung and had to be killed.  The device
callback now reports whether it took the frame, `flush_rx` leaves a refused frame
at the head of the queue and is re-entrancy safe, the relay reads the guest's ack
from every segment and stops draining the socket once 16 KiB is unacknowledged, and
it keeps a 64 KiB tail of unacknowledged data to replay from after a 250 ms pause.
`tests/test_net.c::test_net_user_bulk` pushes 120 KB through a loopback listener
while the fake guest refuses some frames and silently loses some segments, and
asserts the bytes arrive complete and in order; without the relay changes it
delivers 1400 of 120000.

rootfs.yml, tools/mkrootfs.sh: the serial console getty runs -a root.  Asking for
a password there was a dead end rather than a security measure: login verifies
through unix_chkpwd, which reads /etc/shadow only by way of its setgid bit, and
in the released image that path failed (password check failed for root) while
sysvinit's own maintenance prompt accepted the very same password.  A VM console
on your own handset is the maintainer's console, which is what every cloud image
assumes too.  The password still exists for ssh or su, and inittab is asserted so
the flag cannot silently go back.

rootfs.yml: eth0 is manual in /etc/network/interfaces, and the initramfs is what
owns the address.  Having ifupdown run a DHCP client at boot put the login prompt
behind that client's own behaviour -- ISC's dhclient printed its banner, then
blocked before it bound anything, with nothing on the console to say why -- while
the lease rvm's NAT already handed out is kernel state that walks straight into the
new root, address, route and resolver included.  /etc/dhcp/dhclient.conf keeps
short timeouts for anyone who runs dhclient by hand.

virtio: a descriptor chain is now walked to its end.  The iovec table held 16
entries and vq_pop simply stopped collecting when it was full, so any request
bigger than about 56 KB was truncated, the status byte was left unwritten and
the guest waited forever on a request the device considered finished.  Nothing
logged, nothing errored: a Debian boot froze in runlevel S on its first readahead
of 16 pages, and not even SIGTERM reached the process.  Linux puts up to 128
scatter entries in a request unless the device advertises VIRTIO_BLK_F_SEG_MAX,
so the cap is now the queue size, a chain that still overflows it is refused with
S_IOERR instead of guessed at, and test_virtio_blk_long_chain builds 42
descriptors and checks all 40 buffers.

A disk image no longer invites a full fsck on every boot.  /etc/fstab had
passno 1, and a guest that is SIGKILLed by the OS -- which is how a phone stops
this app -- is never cleanly unmounted, so sysvinit checked 2 GiB in silence
before it would show a login prompt.  Both builders now write passno 0,
tools/mkrootfs.sh stops mke2fs setting a mount count or an age, and it stopped
creating the filesystem without a journal: ext4 with no journal is not meant to
survive being unmounted by a signal.

/init seeds the disk's /etc/resolv.conf before handing the console over, because
the lease survives the chroot in kernel state but the resolver config does not,
and it says out loud when DHCP gives it no address.  rootfs.yml enables
networking in rcS.d by hand: Debian's ifupdown postinst does not create a SysV
link, so the shipped image had no eth0 at boot.

Input is no longer parked behind the cursor reply, because parking it was the
half of the fix above that did not work. A new query reset the side buffer, so
every keystroke typed while a prompt was being drawn was thrown away, and a
buffer the guest had only half drained handed its leftovers over a command late
-- which is all "characters appear that nobody typed" ever was. The reply now
goes into the same ring the keystrokes use, so ordering costs nothing and
nothing is held anywhere. pump_input also asks a frontend for no more than the
UART FIFO can still take instead of dropping the difference.

The Android console parses a VT subset instead of swallowing CSI: cursor moves,
ED/EL, IL/DL, insert and delete chars, SGR with the sixteen ANSI colours, save
and restore, and alt-screen as a clear. The parser keeps its state between
writes, which matters because output arrives in 1 KiB batches and a sequence
split across two of them used to print its tail as text. The grid is fixed at 80
columns to match the guest's tty, with the font sized to fit them; deriving the
width from the screen made the app wrap a line where the guest had not.

The phone UI is a terminal now: no button row over the console, no key row while
the keyboard is closed. Boot, Stop, Kernel, Disk, Initrd, Clear and the debug log
are in the header menu; PgUp, PgDn, Home and the arrows are helper keys that ride
with the IME; long-press selects a line and offers Paste, Copy, Select all and
Clear, double-tap copies one.

net_user.c: the resolver forward no longer blocks in recvfrom for two seconds
inside the VM loop (it forwards, returns, and a later poll collects the answer,
which is also the only reason a busybox lookup can win its own timeout), UDP
slots are keyed on destination as well as guest port, and both connection tables
have idle timers -- without them the table filled on the seventeenth lookup and
the network was dead for the rest of the boot. macOS has no MSG_DONTWAIT; the
guard that covers it is what the macos job was failing on.

tools/mkinitrd.sh: /init hands the console over to the disk -- /dev, /proc and
/sys moved with mount --move, then chroot to /sbin/init -- when the disk has one,
so kernel plus disk lands in Debian instead of a busybox shell with no apt.
busybox switch_root is deliberately not used: it printed its usage here and left
no reason behind, which is a bad thing to discover over a serial line.
rvm.shell=1 asks for the shell on purpose. .github/workflows/rootfs.yml
gives the image eth0 with DHCP and installs ifupdown, iproute2, isc-dhcp-client,
iputils-ping, curl and wget, so the guest has a route to that NAT. kernel.yml
publishes initrd.img beside the Image and release.yml says what each file is.

make: a release build carries no -g. The debug info was 257 KB on a 92 KB
binary and PLAN's 300 KB ceiling had no check, so it sat at 340 KB unnoticed;
the number is asserted in build.yml now.

The terminal answers the cursor-position query now. ash asks where the
cursor is (ESC[6n) before drawing its first prompt and blocks until
someone replies; nobody did, so the shell sat dead until the first
keystroke, which it then ate as the answer -- every character typed
before that landed in the wrong place. The emulator tracks the column it
has printed and replies the way a real terminal does, and flushes output that
ends without a newline -- a prompt is exactly that -- on the poll tick
instead of hoarding it until the next write. Typing echoes within a
tenth of a second again.

Two instruction-level bugs turned out to sit behind "the network does
not work". The FP dispatcher filed the compare group under the wrong
funct7, so every feq/flt/fle -- flt.d among them, which is all busybox
ping needs for its timing maths -- trapped as an illegal instruction and
killed the process with SIGILL. The unit-test harness had the same wrong
encoding baked into its macros, which is why the tests agreed with the
emulator instead of the architecture; both now use the real one, and the
suite covers all six compares. Separately, the medeleg default left the
illegal-instruction bit unset through a shifted-constant typo, so any
fault the decoder refused escalated to M-mode, where nothing handles it,
and the machine spun forever at the kernel entry in a fault loop; the
delegation is fixed, and an undelegated exception now stops the VM
loudly instead of spinning.

virtio-mmio rejected every access that was not exactly four bytes wide.
Linux reads config space one byte at a time (vm_get), so the first MAC
read faulted the guest kernel the moment virtio_net probed. The
transport now serves 1/2/4/8-byte windows over both registers and config
space, which is what the spec has always allowed drivers to assume.

The userspace NAT computed its IP and UDP/TCP checksums with u16 loads,
which on a little-endian host produces a byte-swapped checksum: the
guest silently dropped every packet the emulator sent, ARP excepted
because ARP has no checksum. Checksums now sum big-endian bytes. Its TCP
proxy also connected and received on blocking sockets, freezing the
whole VM for the duration of a handshake; the sockets are non-blocking,
unacknowledged data is left for the guest to retransmit, and poll drains
them from the run loop. With all of that in place the smoke test boots a
Debian kernel, insmods the virtio modules, gets a lease over DHCP, pings
the gateway through the NAT and fetches a page from deb.debian.org.

The CI kernel config grows CONFIG_VIRTIO_NET=y so the published Image
needs no modules for eth0, and the shipped initramfs gives udhcpc the
lease script it has always needed -- without one it obtains an address
and never applies it.

The console went black. RvmView paints white glyphs and a white block
cursor on pure black, the header, the button row and the key bar follow,
and the whole app reads like the terminal it is. Termux got the palette
right years ago; there is no reason to argue with it.

init=/bin/sh left the kernel command line. It was a crutch from before
the rootfs had an init, and it cost the guest its tty: no getty, no
login, no echo while typing, no job control, just a shell bolted onto
the console. With sysvinit and a ttyS0 getty in the rootfs the boot ends
at a login prompt, and the initramfs /init gets the same tty behaviour
from setsid cttyhack. The rootfs also grows an /etc/network/interfaces
with eth0 on dhcp, so the userspace NAT configures itself at boot.

The busybox initramfs is back, and this time it ships inside the APK as
an asset with an Initrd button to replace it: a kernel alone boots to a
shell with a working tty and a configured eth0. tools/mkinitrd.sh builds
it, and tools/smoke.sh proves the whole thing the way a user would --
boot under a pty, type at the shell one character at a time, require the
guest to echo while typing, then ping and fetch a page through the NAT.

The kernel loader handles the format Debian actually ships. Debian's riscv64
/boot/vmlinux-* is not an ELF: the EFI stub wraps the boot Image as a PE32+
executable, so the file starts with MZ and carries COFF sections. loader_sniff
picks ELF, PE or raw blob from the first bytes, and loader_load_pe maps the
sections and enters at the start of .text, because offset 0 is the DOS header
and executing it leaves ra unset. Verified against linux-image-6.12.107 from
deb.debian.org: the image loads and executes its own head code up to the satp
write. Full boot is still open, see PLAN step 8.

vm_new sets medeleg and mideleg to the OpenSBI defaults. A kernel entered in
S-mode with medeleg zero sends every page fault to M-mode, where nothing
handles it. That is a firmware duty, not a kernel bug.

The Android screen is redrawn. A dark ink header carries the name and the
status and runs under the status bar, so the clock no longer sits on top of the
Boot button. The four actions are flat 44dp buttons with a hairline face and
the framework ripple. The console respects its padding, gets line spacing and a
blinking block cursor. The helper keys scroll sideways instead of being clipped
on a landscape phone. Still no AndroidX, no Material and no bitmaps: the faces
are shape drawables and the only animation is the cursor.

The VM thread runs at NORM priority instead of MAX. It never blocks, and at MAX
it starves the UI thread on a phone's few cores, which reads as the whole app
freezing. A delayed note in the status line now says so when the guest has
produced no output after eight seconds.

The Android app has a debug mode: hold the RVM title and every line of RVM's
own log goes into the console above the guest output. The log reaches Java
through a new rvm_log_set_sink() hook, because stderr is /dev/null on Android.
The run loop also logs the first sixteen traps and then one in a million with
cause, tval, pc and privilege, which is the whole diagnosis for a guest stuck
in a fault loop.

The PE loader enters the image at offset 0, not at the first section. head.S
begins with c.li s4,-13, whose encoding is the ASCII "MZ" UEFI requires,
followed by a jump to _start_kernel; the first section begins at
relocate_enable_mmu, and entering there writes satp from a trampoline table
setup_vm never filled and spins in a page-fault loop at its own 1: label.

tools/fetch_kernel.sh built linux-image-age-6.12.107+deb13-riscv64 because
substr($2, 9) strips four characters too few from linux-image-. The awk now
prints the package name as it appears in the index.


0.1.0
-----

The first version that boots a bare metal guest, passes its own test suite and
builds an installable APK in CI.

The emulator. RV64IMAFDC in C11 with no dependencies beyond libc and libm.
src/cpu has the core, the CSR file, the trap path and the FP unit. src/mmu does
Sv39, Sv48 and Sv57 with superpages and a direct mapped TLB. src/bus is a flat
sorted region table. src/devices has an ns16550a UART, a CLINT driven by the
host clock, a PLIC, the virtio-mmio transport with a split virtqueue, and
virtio-blk. src/loader reads ELF64 and raw blobs and builds the FDT in C.
src/vm assembles the machine, runs the loop and implements the SBI calls.

Tests. 31 suites and 585 assertions against a harness that builds a real bus,
MMU, CPU, CLINT, PLIC and UART and emits guest instructions into it.

Android. One Activity, three custom Views, no third party dependencies at all.
The debug APK is 176 KB and the release APK 152 KB.
The VM runs on its own thread; input reaches it through a lock free ring and
vm.running is atomic. The release APK is 65 KB.

Build. A plain GNU Makefile with MODE=release|debug|sanity, WERROR=1 and
TEST_ARGS. scripts/build.sh, scripts/test.sh and scripts/clean.sh wrap it.
tools/mkrootfs.sh, tools/fetch_kernel.sh and tools/run.sh build and boot a
Debian guest. Five GitHub Actions workflows: build, test, lint, android and
release.

Tracing. RVM_TRACE_FROM and --trace-from open the instruction trace at one
guest PC and drop everything before it.

Fixes worth recording, because each one is easy to walk into again.

CSR_TIME read as zero. clint_tick() advances mtime and returns the MSIP and
MTIP bits it drives. vm_run() and the test harness both assigned that return
value to cpu.hw_time, which csr.c serves for CSR_TIME, so rdtime returned
interrupt bits instead of mtime. The Linux riscv clocksource is rdtime. The
harness had a second copy of the same mistake and also dropped MSIP.

The run loop did 385 ns per instruction. Two things ran on every retired
instruction and neither needed to. pump_input() is a read() syscall on stdin
and the clint refresh is a clock_gettime(). Both are now done every 256
instructions and immediately whenever a device moves an interrupt line. Separately,
best_source() scanned all 64 PLIC sources twice per refresh to answer what is
almost always no source. Together: 2.60 MIPS to 32.39 MIPS on a 20 million
instruction nop loop.

--stats reported throughput 1000 times too low. It computed instructions per
nanosecond and called the result MIPS.

Signed overflow in addw, subw, addiw and mul. RISC-V specifies a wrap, C
specifies undefined behaviour. These now compute in the matching unsigned type.
Found by the sanitizers job.

sext() and imm_clui() shifted negative signed values left. Found by UBSan.

The ELF builder's PUT16, PUT32 and PUT64 macros shifted the caller's
expression, so PUT64(40, 0) shifted a 32 bit int by 32. Found by UBSan.

make coverage had clean as a prerequisite, which under -j deletes build/ while
parallel compiles are writing into it. It now runs two sequential sub-makes.

scripts/*.sh only accepted -j 4, so CI's -j"$(nproc)" was rejected as an
unknown option.

dtb/rvm.dts referenced &plic on a node that carried only a hand written phandle
property, which dtc rejects.

android/settings.gradle.kts declared plugin repositories but no project
repositories, so AGP could not resolve its own aapt2.

android-actions/setup-android@v3 installs the retired tools package by default
and fails without an explicit package list.

No Java source was in the repository at all. .gitignore had an unanchored rvm
pattern, meant for the ./rvm binary at the root, which also matched
android/app/src/main/java/dev/rvm/app/. Gradle reported
compileJavaWithJavac NO-SOURCE, dexed the generated R classes and produced an
APK that installs and then dies with ClassNotFoundException. The release build
was worse: with no code to keep, R8 produced an empty dex that AGP omitted, and
resource shrinking then removed everything the missing code referenced. Both
workflows now assert that classes.dex exists, that both ABIs carry
librvm_jni.so, and that all five classes survived shrinking with their names.
The patterns are anchored.

KeyBar called add() on itself. It extends LinearLayout, whose method is
addView(). This was the only compile error in 890 lines of Java, and it was
found by CI because the Java had never been in front of a compiler before.

android:extractNativeLibs was set in the manifest as well as derived by AGP
from packaging.jniLibs.useLegacyPackaging, which warns.

The Android manifest declared an ACTION_VIEW filter for octet-stream that
nothing handled, and an octet-stream URI cannot say whether it is a kernel or a
disk image. Removed. Kernels and images enter through ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT,
where the user picks the file.

make lint is -fsyntax-only and so never saw two warnings that only appear once
gcc folds constants at -O2. scripts/test.sh grew --werror, which CI uses.
